=== Step 2: your choices ===

  DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash - DeepSeek; the 2-bit GGUFs by ISTA-DAS, UD-Q8_K_XL by Unsloth: a big model that runs on a PC with a 12 GB card and 64 GB of RAM (the two official 2-bit versions: 257 or 274 GB of disk, about 5-7 tokens a second) or, in Unsloth's 508 GB version, on several graphics cards
  [ok] model: DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash
  Its license: DeepSeek's model license: https://huggingface.co/unsloth/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017-GGUF
  [ok] size: IST-DAS-2.25bpw
  Its GGUF files: ISTA-DAS / ISTA-DASLab (huggingface.co/ISTA-DASLab/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017-GSQ-RCO-GGUF), with the DSpark drafter and a small metadata file from Unsloth

  DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, ISTA-DAS 2.25 bpw: a 274 GB download (155 GB of model, 111 GB n-gram table, 8 GB drafter). Quality: about 7.3% above the original model's perplexity (code and agent work about 2-3% above), the better of the two, 17 GB more disk.
  The n-gram table stays on the SSD and is read a few rows for each word: use an NVMe drive for the model files (a slow disk makes every word slower). The same table serves every ISTA-DAS set, so installing another one downloads it once.
  Graphics memory: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 (24 GB) + NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 (24 GB). About 3,718 of the 15,360 experts (37 GB) stay on the cards; the other 11,642 (112 GB) are computed on the CPU from RAM, and the ones RAM cannot hold are read from the SSD when a word needs them.
  Two cards share each layer's matrices (--tp 2): measured about twice as fast at reading prompts.
  That fits this computer: 504 GB of RAM keeps all 112 GB of the CPU's experts cached.
  Measured on an RTX 5070 (12 GB) with 64 GB of RAM and a 3.6 GB/s NVMe drive: answers about 4.7 tokens a second, a 4,000-token prompt read at 121 tokens a second; an RTX 3060 (12 GB) with 46 GB of RAM: about 7.2 tokens a second; four Radeon AI PRO R9700 cards: about 31 tokens a second. Other computers are slower or faster by what their cards, RAM and drive can do.
  Download: 275 GB to fetch (checked against pinned sizes and SHA-256; it resumes if it stops). At 50 MB/s that is 1.5 hours. Files you already have: --gguf-dir DIR.

